          "text": "{\"evidence\":[{\"paragraph_id\":\"p1\",\"quote\":\"The first one corresponds to the sidereal rotation period (or sidereal day ), i.e., the time that the object takes to complete a full rotation around its axis relative to the background stars ( inertial space ). The other type of commonly used \\\"rotation period\\\" is the object's synodic rotation period (or solar day ), which may differ, by a fraction of a rotation or more than one rotation, to accommodate the portion of the object's orbital period around a star or another body during one day.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p2\",\"quote\":\"For gaseous or fluid bodies, such as stars and giant planets , the period of rotation varies from the object's equator to its pole due to a phenomenon called differential rotation . Typically, the stated rotation period for a giant planet (such as Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune) is its internal rotation period, as determined from the rotation of the planet's magnetic field .\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p37\",\"quote\":\"Saturn 0.44002 +0.00130 −0.00091 days (average, deep interior [ v ] ) 0.44401 days (deep interior [ iv ] ) 0.4264 days (equatorial) 0.44335 days (high latitude) 10 h 33 m 38 s + 1 m 52 s − 1 m 19 s [ 11 ] [ 12 ] 0 d 10 h 39 m 22.4 s [ 13 ] 0 d 10 h 13 m 59 s [ 14 ] 0 d 10 h 38 m 25.4 s [ 14 ] 0.439 30 d (10 h 32 m 36 s) [ 10 ]\"}]}"
